UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 21, Lesson 4: Book discussion circle', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A book discussion circle shares ideas by listening, building on others’ points and using evidence rather than only stating likes.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nTurn \"I hated the ending\" into a discussion move with evidence and an invitation.\n\nFEEDBACK\nName a specific ending detail, explain the effect and ask what others thought.\n\nCHECK\nTalking over others or only giving scores without reasons weakens the circle.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nJoin a short discussion circle on a shared text. Record two evidence-based comments you made and one idea you built from someone else.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"A book discussion circle shares ideas by listening, building on others’ points and using evidence rather than only stating likes.","lesson_number":4,"lesson_role":"Speaking and handwriting","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-OLC-01","EN2-HANDW-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Join a short discussion circle on a shared text. Record two evidence-based comments you made and one idea you built from someone else. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using discussion on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Talking over others or only giving scores without reasons weakens the circle.. Correction: Name a specific ending detail, explain the effect and ask what others thought.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Talking over others or only giving scores without reasons weakens the circle. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating discussion on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with discussion, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about discussion with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for discussion. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for discussion. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Name a specific ending detail, explain the effect and ask what others thought","Talking over others or only giving scores without reasons weakens the circle","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material"],"question":"Turn \"I hated the ending\" into a discussion move with evidence and an invitation. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for discussion; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Careful use of circle and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Talking over others or only giving scores without reasons weakens the circle","A book discussion circle shares ideas by listening, building on others’ points and using evidence rather than only","Name a specific ending detail, explain the effect and ask what others thought"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with discussion?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named circle and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply discussion to new material and name the circle and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Talking over others or only giving scores without reasons weakens the circle","Name circle and explain why it matters only, without linking it to discussion"],"question":"Which response best applies discussion with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries discussion. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Point to exact words in the sentence that show discussion and say what they do","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event","Say the writing is good without naming any words","Talking over others or only giving scores without reasons weakens the circle"],"question":"Read: \"When the ferry horn blasted, Omar gripped the railing and laughed.\" Which choice best uses discussion from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill with textual evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Talking over others or only giving scores without reasons weakens the circle","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Name circle and explain why it matters without linking it to any words in the text","Name a specific ending detail, explain the effect and ask what others thought"],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about discussion?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Lengthen the answer with adjectives while keeping the same idea"],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to analyse circle and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess","Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea"],"question":"Which revision actually improves meaning for discussion?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Accurate transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Retype the worked model as the final answer","Change fonts and claim the work is new","Talking over others or only giving scores without reasons weakens the circle","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about discussion"],"question":"Which response shows accurate use of discussion on fresh material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about discussion with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating discussion on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using discussion on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for discussion. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with discussion, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Talking over others or only giving scores without reasons weakens the circle.. Correction: Name a specific ending detail, explain the effect and ask what others thought.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y4-w21-l4","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise discussion.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use discussion with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify circle and explain why it matters.","I can practise listen independently on a new prompt.","I can notice build needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A book discussion circle shares ideas by listening, building on others’ points and using evidence rather than only stating likes.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","A book discussion circle shares ideas by listening, building on others’ points and using evidence rather than only","A guess with no explanation","A claim unrelated to the text"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about discussion with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nI say \"I agree with Sam about the clue, and the paw print also links to the ending because…\" then invite the next speaker.\n\nTRY FIRST\nTurn \"I hated the ending\" into a discussion move with evidence and an invitation.\n\nFEEDBACK\nName a specific ending detail, explain the effect and ask what others thought.","explain":"A book discussion circle shares ideas by listening, building on others’ points and using evidence rather than only stating likes.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Talking over others or only giving scores without reasons weakens the circle. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Discussion circle talk moves.","caption":"Invite the next speaker.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Build on</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">I agree with Sam, and…</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Evidence</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">paw print links to ending</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: I say \"I agree with Sam about the clue, and the paw print also links to the ending because…\" then invite the next speake Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: I say \"I agree with Sam about the clue, and the paw print also links to the ending because…\" then invite the next speake Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.","options":["Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does","A claim unrelated to the text","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no explanation"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about discussion with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nI say \"I agree with Sam about the clue, and the paw print also links to the ending because…\" then invite the next speaker. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on 